<article class="env-theorem" id="05ES">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/05ES">Theorem <span data-tag="05ES">15.91.18</span>.</a> Let $R$ be a ring, and let $f \in R$. Let $\varphi : R \to S$ be a flat ring map inducing an isomorphism $R/fR \to S/fS$. Then the functor </p>
  <div class="equation">
  \[  \text{Mod}_ R \longrightarrow \text{Mod}_ S \times _{\text{Mod}_{S_ f}} \text{Mod}_{R_ f}, \quad M \longmapsto (M \otimes _ R S, M_ f, \text{can})  \]
</div>
  <p> is an equivalence. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      The category appearing on the right side of the arrow is the category of triples $(M', M_1, \alpha _1)$ where $M'$ is an $S$-module, $M_1$ is a $R_ f$-module, and $\alpha _1 : M'_ f \to M_1 \otimes _ R S$ is a $S_ f$-isomorphism, see Categories, Example <a href="/tag/003R" data-tag="003R">4.31.3</a>. Hence this theorem is a special case of Proposition <a href="/tag/05ER" data-tag="05ER">15.91.16</a>. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>